Parallelism on the CPU
Before programming thousands of lockstep cores on a GPU, we must build a mechanical understanding of how modern CPUs execute concurrent and parallel work.

The Three Forms of Parallelism on the CPU

Instruction-Level Parallelism (ILP): Pipelining, superscalar execution units, and out-of-order execution engines overlap independent instructions within a single hardware thread.
Data-Level Parallelism (SIMD): Single Instruction, Multiple Data hardware vectors execute the same arithmetic operation across multiple data lanes simultaneously.
Thread-Level Parallelism (TLP): Multiple OS threads or lightweight user-space fibers run across distinct hardware cores.



SIMD: One Instruction, Multiple Results
Modern CPU instruction sets provide wide vector registers: - x86-64: AVX2 (256-bit registers, 8 x 32-bit floats), AVX-512 (512-bit registers, 16 x 32-bit floats). - ARM: Neon (128-bit registers, 4 x 32-bit floats), SVE/SVE2 (scalable vector lengths). - Portable Vector Libraries: Google Highway provides a C++ library that compiles to target-specific SIMD instructions at compile time without vendor lock-in.
// Explicit AVX2 Vector Addition in C++
#include &lt;immintrin.h&gt;

void vector_add_avx2(const float* a, const float* b, float* c, int n) {
    int i = 0;
    for (; i &lt;= n - 8; i += 8) {
        // Load 8 32-bit floats (256 bits) into vector registers
        __m256 va = _mm256_loadu_ps(&a[i]);
        __m256 vb = _mm256_loadu_ps(&b[i]);
        // Single instruction: 8 additions in 1 CPU cycle
        __m256 vc = _mm256_add_ps(va, vb);
        // Store back to memory
        _mm256_storeu_ps(&c[i], vc);
    }
    // Remainder loop
    for (; i &lt; n; ++i) {
        c[i] = a[i] + b[i];
    }
}


Modern Lightweight Threads vs Classic OS Threads
Classic operating system threads (e.g. pthreads, OpenMP) allocate 1-8 MB stack frames and require kernel context switches taking thousands of CPU cycles. Modern high-concurrency systems favor lightweight green threads / M:N scheduling: - Go Goroutines: 2-4 KB initial stacks, cooperatively scheduled in user space by the Go runtime. - Java Virtual Threads (Project Loom): Continuation-based fibers unmounted on I/O. - Rust Tokio: Zero-cost state machines compiled from async/await driven by a work-stealing executor.

The CUDA Programming Model & GPU Puzzles
GPUs execute programs using the SIMT (Single Instruction, Multiple Threads) model. While SIMD executes one instruction across vector registers on a single CPU core, SIMT executes independent scalar threads grouped into lockstep warps.

Execution Hierarchy

Thread: The basic execution unit executing a kernel instance.
Warp: A group of 32 contiguous threads executed together on a Streaming Multiprocessor (SM). All 32 threads in a warp share the same instruction counter.
Thread Block: A cooperative group of threads (up to 1024) sharing on-chip shared memory (SRAM) and hardware barrier synchronization (__syncthreads()).
Grid: The collection of all thread blocks dispatched across all available SMs on the device.

// Minimal CUDA Vector Addition Kernel
__global__ void vector_add_kernel(const float* __restrict__ a,
                                  const float* __restrict__ b,
                                  float* __restrict__ c,
                                  int n) {
    // Unique global thread index
    int idx = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;
    if (idx &lt; n) {
        c[idx] = a[idx] + b[idx];
    }
}


Warp Divergence
When threads within the same 32-thread warp take different execution branches (e.g. if (threadIdx.x % 2 == 0)), the hardware must serialize the branches: 1. The warp executes the true path while masking out false threads. 2. The warp executes the false path while masking out true threads. 3. Execution time doubles, reducing efficiency by 50%.

