Coalescing: The ~10x Lever
When threads in a warp access global memory, the memory controller inspects their addresses: - Coalesced: Threads 0..31 access sequential 4-byte words. The hardware serves all 32 threads in a single 128-byte cache line transaction (100% bus utilization). - Strided / Uncoalesced: Each thread accesses a different cache line. The hardware issues 32 separate 128-byte transactions to fetch 128 bytes of useful data (3.1% bus utilization!).


The Matrix Transpose Case Study
Transposing an \(N imes N\) matrix requires reading by row (coalesced) and writing by column (uncoalesced):
// Optimized Transpose using Shared Memory to Coalesce Both Reads and Writes
#define TILE_DIM 32
#define BLOCK_ROWS 8

__global__ void transpose_coalesced(float* odata, const float* idata, int width, int height) {
    // Pad shared memory by 1 float to eliminate bank conflicts!
    __shared__ float tile[TILE_DIM][TILE_DIM + 1];

    int x = blockIdx.x * TILE_DIM + threadIdx.x;
    int y = blockIdx.y * TILE_DIM + threadIdx.y;

    // Coalesced read from global DRAM into shared memory
    for (int j = 0; j &lt; TILE_DIM; j += BLOCK_ROWS) {
        if (x &lt; width && (y + j) &lt; height) {
            tile[threadIdx.y + j][threadIdx.x] = idata[(y + j) * width + x];
        }
    }

    __syncthreads();

    // Re-index to transpose inside shared memory
    x = blockIdx.y * TILE_DIM + threadIdx.x;
    y = blockIdx.x * TILE_DIM + threadIdx.y;

    // Coalesced write to global DRAM!
    for (int j = 0; j &lt; TILE_DIM; j += BLOCK_ROWS) {
        if (x &lt; height && (y + j) &lt; width) {
            odata[(y + j) * height + x] = tile[threadIdx.x][threadIdx.y + j];
        }
    }
}

GEMM Worklog I: Naive to Tiled
General Matrix Multiply (\(C = \alpha A B + \beta C\)) is the computational nucleus of modern deep learning. We walk through a step-by-step optimization worklog from naive implementation to shared memory block tiling.

Naive GEMM: Why It Runs at 1.3% Peak
__global__ void gemm_naive(int M, int N, int K, const float* A, const float* B, float* C) {
    int row = blockIdx.y * blockDim.y + threadIdx.y;
    int col = blockIdx.x * blockDim.x + threadIdx.x;

    if (row &lt; M && col &lt; N) {
        float sum = 0.0f;
        for (int k = 0; k &lt; K; ++k) {
            sum += A[row * K + k] * B[k * N + col];
        }
        C[row * N + col] = sum;
    }
}
Bottleneck Analysis: Every inner loop iteration performs 2 global memory loads (from \(A\) and \(B\)) for 2 floating point operations (1 multiply, 1 add = 2 FLOPs). Operational intensity is \(\frac{2 \text{ FLOPs}}{8 \text{ Bytes}} = 0.25 \text{ FLOPs/Byte}\). On an A100 GPU capable of 19.5 TFLOP/s, this limits throughput to \(0.25 \times 1935 \text{ GB/s} \approx 480 \text{ GFLOP/s}\) (~1.3% of theoretical compute peak).


2D Shared Memory Tiling: Climbing to 36.5% Peak
By caching tiles of matrix \(A\) and \(B\) in fast on-chip SRAM (__shared__), threads reuse loaded elements TILE_SIZE times across the thread block:
template&lt;int BLOCK_SIZE&gt;
__global__ void gemm_tiled_smem(int M, int N, int K, const float* A, const float* B, float* C) {
    __shared__ float s_a[BLOCK_SIZE][BLOCK_SIZE];
    __shared__ float s_b[BLOCK_SIZE][BLOCK_SIZE];

    int row = blockIdx.y * BLOCK_SIZE + threadIdx.y;
    int col = blockIdx.x * BLOCK_SIZE + threadIdx.x;
    float sum = 0.0f;

    for (int bk = 0; bk &lt; K; bk += BLOCK_SIZE) {
        s_a[threadIdx.y][threadIdx.x] = A[row * K + (bk + threadIdx.x)];
        s_b[threadIdx.y][threadIdx.x] = B[(bk + threadIdx.y) * N + col];
        __syncthreads();

        for (int k = 0; k &lt; BLOCK_SIZE; ++k) {
            sum += s_a[threadIdx.y][k] * s_b[k][threadIdx.x];
        }
        __syncthreads();
    }
    C[row * N + col] = sum;
}

Tensor Cores & WMMA: The Second Worklog
Starting with the Volta architecture, NVIDIA GPUs include specialized mixed-precision execution hardware: Tensor Cores.

The Tensor Core Advantage

Conventional CUDA cores execute 1 FMA (Fused Multiply-Add = 2 FLOPs) per cycle.
Tensor Cores compute full matrix multiply-accumulate operations (\(D = A imes B + C\)) on small matrix fragments (e.g. \(16 imes 16 imes 16\)) in hardware every cycle.
Speedup: ~5-10x higher theoretical compute density compared to FP32 CUDA cores.



Direct Programming via WMMA (Warp Matrix Multiply Accumulate)
CUDA provides the nvcuda::wmma C++ API to program Tensor Cores directly from device code:
#include &lt;mma.h&gt;
using namespace nvcuda;

// Cooperative 16x16x16 matrix multiply across 32 threads in a warp
__global__ void wmma_gemm_kernel(const half* A, const half* B, float* C, int M, int N, int K) {
    // Declare WMMA fragments
    wmma::fragment&lt;wmma::matrix_a, 16, 16, 16, half, wmma::row_major&gt; a_frag;
    wmma::fragment&lt;wmma::matrix_b, 16, 16, 16, half, wmma::col_major&gt; b_frag;
    wmma::fragment&lt;wmma::accumulator, 16, 16, 16, float&gt; c_frag;

    wmma::fill_fragment(c_frag, 0.0f);

    for (int k = 0; k &lt; K; k += 16) {
        // Load sub-matrices cooperatively across the warp
        wmma::load_matrix_sync(a_frag, A + (blockIdx.y * 16 * K) + k, K);
        wmma::load_matrix_sync(b_frag, B + (blockIdx.x * 16 * K) + k, K);

        // Hardware Tensor Core Matrix Multiply
        wmma::mma_sync(c_frag, a_frag, b_frag, c_frag);
    }

    // Store accumulator fragment back to global memory
    wmma::store_matrix_sync(C + (blockIdx.y * 16 * N) + (blockIdx.x * 16), c_frag, N, wmma::mem_row_major);
}

Verification, Profiling, and Benchmarking Harness
Every kernel submission must pass a three-tiered validation suite:

1. Correctness Verification
Compare kernel output tensors against double-precision PyTorch / FP64 reference outputs across edge-case dimensions, non-multiple-of-32 matrix sizes, and extreme numerical scales (\(10^{-5}\) to \(10^5\)). Maximum allowable relative error:
\[\frac{\|Y_{\text{kernel}} - Y_{\text{ref}}\|_2}{\|Y_{\text{ref}}\|_2} &lt; 10^{-3}\]


2. CUDA Event Benchmarking
Measure kernel execution time using cudaEventRecord over 1,000 warm iterations, discarding outliers:
cudaEvent_t start, stop;
cudaEventCreate(&start);
cudaEventCreate(&stop);

cudaEventRecord(start);
for (int i = 0; i &lt; 1000; ++i) {
    launch_kernel&lt;&lt;&lt;grid, block&gt;&gt;&gt;(...);
}
cudaEventRecord(stop);
cudaEventSynchronize(stop);

float ms = 0;
cudaEventElapsedTime(&ms, start, stop);
printf("Average execution time: %f ms\n", ms / 1000.0f);
